Z||000000||Smokin' GT||11-14-2001||02:48 AM||||Hi guys, I mentioned a while back some info I had found from an article about ram air and why it did not exist and there was a great deal of arguement over the issue.  Since the question is still in controversy (people who want to sell their holley scoops for $100 might not benefit from this) I found the article I was speaking of, it was on the fiero secrects site actually.<P>the site is  <A HREF="http://www.gemair.com/~phorine/Articles-Ram%20Air%20Myth.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gemair.com/~phorine/Articles-Ram%20Air%20Myth.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>-87 Bright Blue Metallic GT-<BR>          -Joel||||reg||4
Z||000001||Nashco||11-14-2001||03:04 AM||||1FST2M6 (Trav) has used a side scoop, the "shop vac" looking one that Tigger has on his car, I believe.  I can't remember the specifics exactly, but I believe that with an estimated 30 mph wind Trav got about .3 horse.<P>I don't think "ram air" *adds* any horsepower, just makes up for inadequacies in your intake system.  Basically makes up for what you were losing, so seems like adding power.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||Smokin' GT||11-14-2001||03:13 AM||||right, it could only really be "redirecting" cooler, more dense air to the intake.  It's not adding horsepower, or speeding up airflow, just feeding cooler air to the warm engine compartment. <P>------------------<BR>-87 Bright Blue Metallic GT-<BR>          -Joel||||reg||1
Z||000003||avengador1||11-14-2001||08:56 AM||||How do you think they got the extra 20 H.P. on the Camaro and Firebird. These engines were 300 H.P. before they added the "ram Air". The scoop is in a high pressure area on the hood where cool air is. If you can get a less restrictive air flow with cooler denser air you will generate more H.P.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||avengador1||11-14-2001||09:04 AM||||As for the article you mention. Air is incompressible is not true. Water is incompressible. If you cool air down it becomes more dense and takes up less room, in effect "compressing". If your engine is using the air from the engine compartment, this air is hotter and less dense than the outside air, so you get less air in the mixture. Especially if you have the restrive factory intake. If you can redirect cooler outside air with a less restrive air intake, you will have more air to add to the combustion process. If you add more fuel to this, then you will have more H.P.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jncomutt||11-14-2001||09:13 AM||||I have to go with my CT bud on this.  Hes right.  Air is compressable.  (take a turkey bastor, or large sirenge or such, cover the hold at the end and sqeeze the ball, or push the piston thing; notice how it moves?)  Why do you think its soo bad to have air in a hydrolic line.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||lou_dias||11-14-2001||09:17 AM||||that's what turbos and SC's do, throw compressed air into your engine that's how intercoolers get more hp out of them.<P>The cooler the air, easier to compress because compressing air causes heat as a side effect||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||11-14-2001||09:56 AM||||The problem with the article is his definition of "Ram Air" is reference to jet engines. Air traveling thru a scoop isn't "compressed" but it is cooled and provides a positive air pressure. When the throttle plate is opened the cooler air is drawn in. This allows the atomized fuel to stay atomized longer rather than vaporized. The result is a better burn rate and increased pressure. This puts more power behind the piston resulting in increased horsepower. This is the same principle as why using an inter-cooler increases horsepower on turbo engines. The inter-cooler cools the air and the turbo compresses it all in the cylinders. At 60mph a scoop (like the Holley or other side scoop) doesn't provde any actual "boost" but provides better quality of air. There is an actual gain in performance (no matter how small, it is still a gain) and economy at highway speeds is the other benefit. Think of the scoop as a poor man's inter-cooler. You are by definition ramming cooler air into the engine. That is how the automotive field defines "ram air". <P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">          <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000008||1FST2M6||11-14-2001||10:38 AM||||i got a better reading with a 30mph wind.. i was also reading .02bar or .29 psi. in the pelenum. the NACA duct that Pontiac put on the car works ok but not as well as Brents. i was able to reproduce similar results with the stock intake grill but at a very very unlikley wind angle nearly 45* from the car with the same wind -so if your drifting through a  corner you'll make  .4more hp @ 30mph... the Holley scoop is just a peice of plastic with a name on it. at a given speed the air pressure is higher and as with any forced induction engine be it turbo or higher speed ram air on a TA the more air pressure the more torque your going to make. i've got dyno proof of that... as for fluid dynamics... well I've been working on my ME degree for  over 5 years now and what the article states is correct... about how air flows.. but not on how engines act. the ram air car like our compensate for air volume, air pressure, intake temp, pelenum air temps and stoich effeicency is 14.7:1 few cars see this area the computer having to compensate for all this forcing a car to react in a positive manor with the added intake volume and pressure at speed.   <P>just my $.02||||reg||1
Z||000009||Slammed Fiero||11-14-2001||10:52 AM||||When I worked at GM in marketing  They told me they just revived the "Ram Air" name because the target market for the car had a stong connection with the phrase having grown up in the 60's (25 to 40 year old target market)<P><BR>As for it making the 30hp on the cars in which it is used...Sorry , it's the exhaust and computer.<P>If anyone remember Steve huskins From a few years back on the Fiero.org list he explained why it is more or less a sham more than once. And with a degree in Aerospace engineering , and various other automotive accolades I tend to believe him. HE still has the car , although it's outta commission , and works testing jet engines for Pratt & Whitney.<P>He had all sorts of calculations pertaining to this. If I remeber correctly you would have to drive 300mph to see any real gain in HP.<P>I will see if I can't track him down , he is on ICQ once in a while.<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||11-14-2001||10:59 AM||||thats also the reason for intercoolers on turbo cars.......more cool air||||reg||1
Z||000011||Formula88||11-14-2001||05:07 PM||||"Ram Air" as used by Pontiac is a marketing term.  Ram Air as referenced by the aerospace industry refers to the Ram Air effect for a Ram Jet.  This effect can't happen at street speeds.<P>Whose correct?  Both, and neither.  You aren't talking about the same things.  Ram Air does provide a performance increase, but not by causing a Ram Air effect.  It puts the air intake into a higher pressure air stream, so you see slightly elevated intake air pressure - but the primary advantage is that it puts the air intake into a cool air stream.  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||GTFiero1||11-14-2001||06:15 PM||||i dont know. i recently saw a thing on them special SLP ram air firbirds and th ram air added 15hp, exhaust was an option. a rea ram air has a seal around the air filter to seal the gap between the hood and filter usually that forces air into the intake, adding hp, not as much of a turbo or supercharger though. the scoops or holly scoops we can put on the fiero dont really do much unless you connect the intake a filter directly up to the scoop, or build and air box or somethin.<P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.||||reg||1
Z||000013||avengador1||11-14-2001||07:46 PM||||The air intakes on the Firebird and Camaro are "tuned" air intakes. They are designed to allow the least amount of restriction to the air entering the engine. They also are isolated from the engine compartment and don't add heat to the incoming air. The air is not rammed in per se it just has a freer path in. The scoops are usually put in a high pressure area of the grille or hood, so in theory, the faster you go the more air will be pushed in the scoops, hence the name "Ram Air". By being "Tuned" air intakes they help the engine make more H.P. Have you ever seen an engine being dyno tested? Did you notice the big velocity stack they put on it to speed up the air flow in? They don't use an air filter to do this test because they are going for max H.P. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||ray b||11-14-2001||11:21 PM||||ALL F-1 cars use big scoops for air if they don't work [bs] why do they put up with the drag and razed cg???<BR>ram air works just look at the dragracers they all are used on all the pro-racers cars.<BR>they may not be alot of help at street speeds or in holly scoops but done right they work as if it don't racers move on to things that do work.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||11
Z||000015||Posthumane||11-15-2001||12:18 AM||||Ray, the big scoops on the side of an F1 car are the radiator pods, not engine air intake. If your refering to the intake above the driver, the reason they put it there isn't strictly because of the ram air effect. It's there bacuse it is integrated with the roll hoop, and provides an easy path for the air to flow to the engine which is directly behind the driver.<BR>"Ram air" as is currently used by pontiac and such is just their name for a cold air intake system. It's no different than those big pipes going to the fender on riced hondas and such.||||reg||1
Z||000016||ray b||11-15-2001||01:23 AM||||that big scoop behind the drivers heads on F-1 cars is for ram air,roll bars are just over helmet hight by rule and part of head rest NOT IN SCOOPS look at a exploded pic/drawings of the cars.<BR>and dragracers use them toooo pro stock to AA/fuel use big scoops that are no were near roll bars!!!!<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||11
Z||000017||Mach10||11-15-2001||02:10 AM||||The article was, from my investigations, correct, but for the wrong reasons. The idea is that the air would not have enough momentum to "compress" itself through the system. Basically, once it reaches a certain pressure level, it just bounces around it. At street speeds, this pressure level is pretty low. I seriously doubt that you'd be getting .29psi at the intake. Especially after the air filter... But hey... I've never hooked up a gauge and tried  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> That's why not all the crap on your floors starts flying around if you only open ONE window. <P>However, it DOES provide cooler air. That would explain the dyno-results. Oh, and GM's Ramair naming on the camaros/firechickens is BS. It's a tuned intake. That's different  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000018||Smokin' GT||11-15-2001||02:25 AM||||So what a couple of you are saying is that when the car is at a standstill it only has 300 h.p. but then all the sudden when you're moving there's 30 more horsepower flowing through the engine?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  Well if some silly scoop could add me 30 horsepower where do I sign up?! :WTF:<P>------------------<BR>-87 Bright Blue Metallic GT-<BR>          -Joel||||reg||5
Z||000019||Ali_Gulf_Fiero||11-16-2001||09:47 AM||||The big airboxes on formula cars (and also on prototypes like the Porsche 936 of the 70's)are there not for Ram Air(that's what forced induction is for) - they are there to get the intake out of the turbulent airflow that exists close to the skin of the body and aft of protrusions, the largest of which being the exposed tires. The only way to use a flush intake without it being starved is through the use of a carefully designed duct of the recessed triangular NACA variety. The supposed "NASA style" hood scoops on the early 70's Mustang Mach1s and revived for aftermarket late model Mustang replacement hoods are bullsh!t. On Fieros, I say use scoops of the Lambo Diablo/Murcielago type. Don't worry about starvation, and don't think about Ram Air. At typical Fiero speeds, the car won't see either.<P>------------------<BR>my cat's sitting in my scrambled eggs.||||reg||1
Z||000020||grinthock||11-16-2001||10:17 AM||||So much hostility, all I know is with a scantool the MAP sensor saw a voltage increase on the highway at the same speed under the same conditions such as weather and the like, I saw a 5% voltage increase.||||reg||1
